The Operating System (OS) acts as a bridge between the user and the computer hardware. It makes it easy for users to interact with the computer without needing to understand or write complex hardware-level instructions. Instead of directly controlling components like the CPU, memory, or disk, users perform simple actions, and the OS handles the rest.
For example, when a user clicks on a file to open it, the mouse input is detected by the hardware and passed to the operating system. The OS then understands this request and locates the file on the storage device (hard disk or SSD). It reads the file data from the disk and loads it into RAM, which is a faster temporary memory used for processing.
After the data is loaded into RAM, the CPU executes the required instructions to process the file. Finally, the OS sends the output to the display so the user can see the file content.
In this way, the operating system manages all hardware components and makes communication between the user and the computer simple, efficient, and possible.
